course: everyday
lesson: 278
title: Honest schoolwork, kind jokes and pranks that harm
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A test is not a race against the teacher; it shows what you know so the teacher can help with what you don't. Copying, forging a parent's signature or keeping a mark you did not earn all hide the truth that the test is meant to find.
explain: Jokes are for sharing a laugh. Silly knock-knock jokes with a little brother build friendship and help him learn words. But some words are never jokes: bombs, weapons and threats at airports or schools must be treated seriously by staff, and can cause alarm, searches and serious trouble.
explain: Teasing turns unkind when it picks on who someone is: their accent, the language they speak at home, or what they like doing. Speaking two languages is a skill, and everyone may like dancing or football.
explain: A prank stops being funny when someone pays for it: damaged property, a frightened pet, a scared neighbour, or a friend who cannot go home. Example: a boy hides his friend's shoes as a joke, the friend misses the bus and his mum is worried sick. The «joke» is only funny to one person.
fact: In a test, keep your eyes on your own paper; the test finds out what you know so the teacher can help you.
fact: Never sign a note or reading log for a parent; forging a signature is a serious lie that breaks trust with school and family.
fact: If a teacher adds up your mark wrongly in your favour, tell them; honesty about mistakes that help you shows real integrity, which means being honest even when nobody would find out.
fact: Playing along with a younger child's silly jokes builds friendship and helps them learn language.
fact: Never joke about bombs, weapons or threats, especially at airports or schools; staff must treat such words seriously.
fact: Speaking more than one language is a skill, and mocking someone's accent is unkind.
fact: If an older sibling teases you constantly, tell them how it makes you feel, and ask a parent for help if it continues.
fact: Everyone can like what they like; teasing about interests makes people hide what they enjoy.
fact: Never damage property in a prank; someone has to clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime.
fact: Never prank animals; they do not understand jokes and get frightened, stressed or hurt.
fact: Ringing doorbells and running away can frighten people, and running near roads is dangerous.
fact: Do not hide things people need, like shoes or a bag; they could be late, get in trouble or be really worried.
fact: Saying or writing that two people love each other, faking a love note, or passing on rumours about someone's private life can embarrass and hurt them, so it is not a harmless joke.
exercise: During a spelling test, the boy next to you has his answers showing. What should you do? | Keep your eyes on your own paper | Copy only the hard words | Copy them but change the spelling a bit | Ask him to move his paper closer
exercise: Why does copying in a test hurt the copier? | The teacher cannot see what you really need help with | The copied answers are always wrong | Teachers give lower marks to neat work | It makes the test take longer for everyone in the room
exercise: You forgot to get your reading log signed. What is right? | Tell the teacher and get your parent to sign it later | Sign it yourself in your parent's handwriting, very neatly | Ask an older friend to sign it | Tear the page out of the log
exercise: Why is forging a parent's signature serious? | It is a lie that breaks trust with school and family | It wastes ink and paper | Only the teacher is allowed to sign logs | It is only serious if you get caught doing it by the teacher
exercise: Your teacher added your marks wrong and gave you 18 instead of 15. What should you do? | Tell the teacher about the mistake | Keep quiet because it was her mistake | Tell your friends but not the teacher | Ask for 20 since she is generous
exercise: Your little sister tells a knock-knock joke that makes no sense. What is kind? | Play along and laugh with her | Tell her it is a stupid joke | Explain carefully why it is not funny and how a real joke works | Ignore her until she stops
exercise: Why is it good to share silly jokes with younger children? | It builds friendship and helps them learn language | It makes them stop asking so many questions all day long | It teaches them never to try telling a joke ever again | It proves to everyone that you are funnier than them
exercise: At the airport in Chicago, a boy says loudly «I've got a bomb in my bag!» as a joke. What can happen? | Staff must treat it seriously: alarm, searches, trouble | Everyone laughs and the plane leaves early | Nothing, because children are allowed to joke at airports | The family gets moved to first class
exercise: A classmate speaks with a Polish accent and some kids copy her voice. What should you do? | Stand up for her or stay with her | Join in so they don't copy you next | Tell her to change her accent | Laugh quietly but say nothing
exercise: Why is mocking someone's accent unkind? | Speaking more than one language is a skill | Accents always sound the same | Only adults have accents | It is only unkind if the teacher hears you doing it in class
exercise: Your older brother teases you every single day. What is a good first step? | Tell him how it feels; get a parent's help if it goes on | Tease him back twice as hard | Hide his things to get revenge | Keep it all inside and never tell anyone how much it hurts
exercise: Kids laugh at a boy in Glasgow because he loves ballet. What is right? | Do not join in; everyone can like what they like | Join in so you fit in with the group | Tell him to choose a different hobby that boys usually like | Laugh, but only behind his back
exercise: Some teenagers plan to throw eggs at a house on Halloween. Why is this wrong? | Someone must clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime | Eggs are too expensive to waste | It is only wrong if the house is new | It is fine because it is Halloween and everyone does it
exercise: A boy wants to jump out and scare the family cat for a video. What is right? | Never prank animals; they get frightened | It is fine if the video is funny | Cats like being surprised | Do it once so the cat learns to be braver next time
exercise: Why is «ding dong ditch» (ringing doorbells and running away) a bad idea? | It can frighten people, and running near roads is dangerous | Doorbells break after a few rings | It is fine if nobody answers | It is only bad in the daytime, when people are awake to hear it
exercise: A girl hides her friend's shoes just before home time. What could happen? | The friend could be late, in trouble or really worried | The friend will think it is very funny | Nothing, because shoes are always found quickly by the teachers | The teacher will give her a prize
test: In a maths test you see that your friend wrote a different answer from yours. What is honest? | Keep your own answer; the test is about what you know | Change yours to match hers | Rub both out and leave it blank | Whisper to ask which is right
test: A boy in Melbourne lost a permission slip and thinks of writing his dad's name on a new one. What should he do instead? | Ask his dad to sign it, even if it means being late | Sign it neatly so it looks real | Ask his big sister to copy the signature from an old note | Tell the teacher his dad already signed
test: Which shows real integrity? | Telling the teacher she gave you too many points | Keeping extra points when nobody notices | Asking a friend to check your teacher's adding | Only reporting the mistakes that lower your mark
test: At school, a girl says «I'm going to blow up the classroom!» as a joke. Why is it a problem? | Staff must treat threats seriously, even as a joke | It is only a problem if she has matches | Jokes in class are always allowed | Nobody ever listens to what children say in a classroom anyway
test: A new boy speaks Spanish at home and English at school. Which is TRUE? | Speaking two languages is a real skill | It means that his English is wrong and weak | He should only speak one language, English | It is fine to tease him about it a little
test: Which prank is fine? | A silly harmless joke that everyone laughs at, the friend too | Stuffing toilet paper in a school toilet | Spraying paint on a neighbour's fence | Locking the dog in a cupboard for a laugh
test: A girl plays football and some boys say it is «not for girls». What is right? | Everyone can like what they like; don't join the teasing | Agree, because football is only for boys | Tell her to try a girls' game instead, like netball | Stay out of it and laugh along
test: Why should you never scare a dog for a prank video? | The dog does not understand jokes and gets frightened | Dogs always find it funny | It ruins the video lighting | Only cats get scared, dogs enjoy a surprise and a loud noise
test: Your sister keeps calling you names despite you asking her to stop. What now? | Ask a parent to help her learn where the line is | Call her even worse names back until she finally stops | Break something of hers so she knows how it feels | Stop talking to everyone at home until she says sorry
test: can i just sign mums name on the reading log, she's busy | No, forging her signature is a serious lie | Yes, if you really did all the reading on the log | Yes, schools never check the signatures anyway | Only if you copy her handwriting really neatly
test: teacher added my marks wrong, i got 5 extra. say sumthing? | Yes, tell her, honesty counts even when it helps you | No, it was her mistake and not yours, so just keep quiet | No, keep it, it all evens out later in the year anyway | Only if a friend noticed it too and might tell on you
test: gonna hide my sisters shoes before school haha | Don't, she could be late, in trouble or worried | Go on, it's a harmless joke | Fine, as long as you hide them well | Fine, if you tell her at lunchtime where they are
test: wanna scare the cat with a cucumber for a vid, ok? | No, animals don't get jokes and get scared | Yes, cats find it funny afterwards and forget all about it | Yes, if the video gets lots of views | Only if the cat is asleep first
explain: A boy in Canada joked online that he would bring a gun to school; the police came to his home, the school was locked down, and he was suspended even though he never meant it.
explain: A girl in Wales signed her mum's name on a school form; when the school found out, she lost a school trip and it took a long time for her mum to trust her again.
fact: Threats about weapons or bombs are always taken seriously by schools and police, even when the child says it was a joke.
fact: Copying in a test hides what you do not know, so the gaps grow and the work gets harder later.
test: said a bomb joke in class and now teacher is calling the office?? | Tell the truth and say sorry, such jokes are taken seriously | Deny you ever said it | Laugh so they know it was nothing | Say a friend said it instead
test: forgot to get my reading log signed, just sign it myself? | No, tell the teacher and get it signed tonight | Yes, nobody ever checks the signatures on reading logs | Yes, if you copy the name well enough to look real | Yes, just this once, as long as you did the reading
test: teacher added my mark wrong, i got 5 extra lol | Tell the teacher, honesty matters even when it helps | Keep quiet, it was her mistake, so it's not your problem | Tell your friends but not her | Ask her to add more marks
test: did u hear a kid forged his mum's signature on his report and "the school called the police". lesson? | Never forge a parent's signature; it breaks trust | Forging is fine as long as the writing looks real enough | Only sign it yourself for small notes, never for reports | Get a friend to sign it instead, then it isn't you
test: my brother says someone hid a boy's shoes and he "had to walk home barefoot in the snow for 3 miles". what's prob exaggerated | The snow and the miles; hiding things people need still hurts | That shoes are needed | That hiding things is a prank | That the boy was upset
test: kids are saying the new girl "talks weird" cos of her accent and want to copy her voice at lunch. should u join in | No, mocking an accent is unkind | Yes, it's just a bit of fun | Yes, if she can't hear you | Yes, accents are meant to be copied
test: in class someone writes "Leo loves Ava" on the board and everyone laughs. Ava looks upset. what's kind? | Don't laugh; rub it off or tell the teacher | Add a heart to make it funnier | Write your own name next to it | Laugh along, everyone's doing it so it can't be that bad
test: "let's pretend Zoe sent Max a love note, it'll be hilarious" — is that a harmless prank? | No; it can embarrass both of them | Yes, love notes are always funny to everyone | Yes, if you sign it with Zoe's name | Yes, if you only show it to the class
test: mate said "my dad says the teacher only got the job cos she's going out with the head" — is that fair to repeat? | No; it's a rumour about someone's private life | Yes, dads always know | Yes, but only to your friends | Yes, tell the teacher to her face so she knows what people say
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a classroom test, by reason of which a pupil keeps their eyes upon their own paper. | It shows what the pupil knows, so the teacher may assist | It ranks pupils for the purpose of punishment | It determines which pupils may attend school | It measures the speed of a pupil's handwriting
exam: Whereas a teacher has erroneously totalled a mark in the pupil's favour, select the response consistent with integrity. | The pupil shall inform the teacher of the error | The pupil shall retain the mark in silence | The pupil shall inform only their friends | The pupil shall request a further increase in recognition of honesty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treatment by schools and police of threats concerning weapons or bombs? | Such threats are always taken seriously, even if described as a joke | Such threats are disregarded when made by children | Such threats are permitted provided no weapon exists | Such threats are matters solely for the parents
exam: State the principal objection to pranks played upon animals. | Animals do not comprehend jokes and become frightened or hurt | Animals are entitled by law to retaliate | Such pranks are prohibited only upon farms | Animals object only to pranks played at night
